---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import me from '@/assets/me.jpg';
---

<section id="about" class="container-page py-28" aria-labelledby="about-title">
  <div class="grid items-start gap-14 lg:grid-cols-[22rem_1fr]">
    <figure class="reveal mx-auto w-full max-w-xs lg:sticky lg:top-28">
      <Image
        src={me}
        alt="Portrait of Jinesh Soni"
        widths={[320, 480, 640]}
        sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 352px, 320px"
        class="aspect-[4/5] w-full rounded-2xl object-cover"
      />
      <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">Based in India · works remotely</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h2 id="about-title" class="mt-4 text-[clamp(2.4rem,5vw,3.6rem)] leading-[1.02]">
        I started by breaking my own phone. <em>Then I learned to build.</em>
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        <p>
          Around 2012, in university, I was{' '}
          <strong class="font-medium text-ink">modding Android ROMs</strong> to squeeze more
          performance out of my phone — and writing about it on my tech blog,{' '}
          <a class="link" href="https://techcure.wordpress.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            TechCure
          </a>
          . It grew to 400K+ visits and taught me Android and Java inside out. In 2014 I started
          building apps professionally and never looked back.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since then I’ve worked across healthcare, crypto, travel, agri-tech and ed-tech — first as
          an Android engineer, then leading mobile teams, and today as Principal Developer at
          AppExert, architecting an AI-powered reading app with on-device speech recognition.
        </p>
        <p>
          The thread through all of it: apps for people who don’t have perfect conditions. Traders
          on budget phones in busy mandis. Kids reading aloud in classrooms with flaky Wi-Fi. I like
          the constraints — they make the engineering honest.
        </p>
        <p>
          And increasingly, the work is AI. Not demos — AI that has to hold up in the same messy
          conditions. At Readable English that meant running speech recognition{' '}
          <strong class="font-medium text-ink">entirely on-device</strong> with sherpa-onnx across
          iOS, Android and the web, writing the matching algorithm that turns a recogniser’s guesses
          into word-accurate reading feedback, and building an AI pipeline that generates learning
          content levelled to each reader’s Lexile and grade. At Bijak it was TensorFlow models and
          Gen AI tooling that quietly took manual work off the operations team.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next, I’m exploring where{' '}
          <strong class="font-medium text-ink">agents, RAG and local LLMs</strong> fit into mobile
          products — small models that run on the device, respect privacy, and keep working when the
          network doesn’t.
        </p>
      </div>
      <dl class="mt-10 grid max-w-2xl gap-6 border-t border-line pt-6 text-sm sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <dt class="text-muted">Education</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1">B.E. Computer Science, Gujarat Technological University</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-muted">Languages</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1">English, Hindi</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
